  "content": "<div id=\"readability-page-1\" class=\"page\"><div id=\"\"> <p>APOD</p> <p>Astronomy Picture of the Day</p> <p> <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a> Each day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is featured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer. </p> <div><p><img width=\"2000\" height=\"1255\" src=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/5SNR_Auriga_2000.jpg?w=2000&amp;h=1255&amp;fit=clip&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" srcset=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/5SNR_Auriga_2000.jpg?w=2000&amp;h=1255&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 2000w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/5SNR_Auriga_2000.jpg?w=300&amp;h=188&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 300w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/5SNR_Auriga_2000.jpg?w=768&amp;h=482&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 768w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/5SNR_Auriga_2000.jpg?w=1024&amp;h=643&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1024w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/5SNR_Auriga_2000.jpg?w=1536&amp;h=964&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1536w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/5SNR_Auriga_2000.jpg?w=400&amp;h=251&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 400w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/5SNR_Auriga_2000.jpg?w=600&amp;h=377&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 600w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/5SNR_Auriga_2000.jpg?w=900&amp;h=565&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 900w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/5SNR_Auriga_2000.jpg?w=1200&amp;h=753&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1200w\" sizes=\"(max-width: 2000px) 100vw, 2000px\"><img width=\"2000\" height=\"1255\" src=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/5SNR_Auriga_annotated_2000.jpg?w=2000&amp;h=1255&amp;fit=clip&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" srcset=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/5SNR_Auriga_annotated_2000.jpg?w=2000&amp;h=1255&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 2000w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/5SNR_Auriga_annotated_2000.jpg?w=300&amp;h=188&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 300w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/5SNR_Auriga_annotated_2000.jpg?w=768&amp;h=482&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 768w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/5SNR_Auriga_annotated_2000.jpg?w=1024&amp;h=643&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1024w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/5SNR_Auriga_annotated_2000.jpg?w=1536&amp;h=964&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1536w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/5SNR_Auriga_annotated_2000.jpg?w=400&amp;h=251&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 400w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/5SNR_Auriga_annotated_2000.jpg?w=600&amp;h=377&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 600w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/5SNR_Auriga_annotated_2000.jpg?w=900&amp;h=565&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 900w, https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/september/5SNR_Auriga_annotated_2000.jpg?w=1200&amp;h=753&amp;fit=crop&amp;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint 1200w\" sizes=\"auto, (max-width: 2000px) 100vw, 2000px\"></p></div> <div> <p><strong>Explanation:</strong> The ghosts of five supernovas haunt this extraordinary <a href=\"https://app.astrobin.com/i/jvnafq?r=D\">image</a>. It was acquired at <a href=\"https://www.planetary.org/space-images/morocco-oukameden-sky-survey\">Oukaïmeden Observatory</a> in <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/wet-and-dry-morocco-144677/\">Morocco</a> with approximately 200 hours of observations and shows a large patch of the sky, equivalent to the <a href=\"https://lco.global/spacebook/sky/using-angles-describe-positions-and-apparent-sizes-objects/\">area</a> of one thousand full moons tiled together, in the constellation of <a href=\"https://noirlab.edu/public/education/constellations/auriga/\">Auriga</a> (the <a href=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1706793987472-2e3b3ec44fcc\">charioteer</a>). From left to right, the five supernova remnants visible across the field are <a href=\"https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2017/01/aa29848-16\">G181.1+9.5</a>, <a href=\"https://articles.adsabs.harvard.edu/pdf/1998A%26A...331..661K\">G182.4+4.3</a>, <a href=\"https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/link_gateway/2018MNRAS.478.1987H/PUB_HTML\">G179.0+2.6</a>, <a href=\"https://academic.oup.com/mnras/article/448/4/3196/955844\">G180.0−1.7</a> (Sh2-240, the <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap240227.html\">Spaghetti Nebula</a>), and <a href=\"https://www.aanda.org/articles/aa/full_html/2011/08/aa17179-11/aa17179-11.html\">G178.2−4.2</a>. They are highlighted in the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-september-24-the-ghosts-of-five-supernovas/image/2609/5SNR_Auriga_annotated_1000.jpg\">annotated image</a>, together with <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap250227.html\">open cluster</a> <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap100305.html\">M37</a> and the <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap260317.html\">Tadpole Nebula</a>. As each explosion <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap250108.html\">expanded into space</a>, it created a growing shell of shocked gas and delicate filamentary structures shown in red (<a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap050930.html\">hydrogen</a>) and blue (<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doubly_ionized_oxygen\">oxygen</a>), respectively. These ancient stellar explosions happened independently; they are at various distances up to about several thousands of <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/exoplanets/what-is-a-light-year/\">light-years</a> away from Earth and have estimated ages up to <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/ap260107.html\">tens of thousands of years old</a>. Early humans may have seen them as bright new stars, fading over weeks or months.</p><p><strong>APOD's email for image submissions has changed.</strong> Please see: <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/apod/lib/apsubmit2015.html\">APOD Submissions.</a><br><strong>APOD's main NASA site is <a href=\"https://asterisk.apod.com/viewtopic.php?t=45023\">moving</a> </strong>: From <a href=\"https://apod.nasa.gov/\">apod.nasa.gov</a> to <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod\">science.nasa.gov/apod</a><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture: </strong>what's next?</p></div> </div></div>",
